---
import Recording from "@/components/Recording.astro"
import DocsFaq from "@/components/DocsFaq.astro"
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro"
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
import { docsMetadata } from "@/lib/docs-meta"
import { faqPageJsonLd } from "@/lib/faq"

const metadata = docsMetadata("/docs/usage/results")


const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How do I view my Gooze mutation results?",
    a: "Run `gooze report view`. It reads the reports directory and prints a per-file score table, one line per survivor, and the mutation score. Add `--all` to list every mutation with its diff (an interactive list in a TTY, plain text in CI).",
  },
  {
    q: "What does a `survived` mutation mean in Gooze?",
    a: "A survived mutation means Gooze injected a bug but the package's tests still passed, usually because of a weak or missing assertion. Add a test that would fail on that behavior change.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does a `killed` mutation mean?",
    a: "A killed mutation is the good case: the tests of the package owning the mutated file failed for the mutation, so your tests caught the change.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does a `timeout` status mean in Gooze?",
    a: "The mutant's tests exceeded `--mutation-timeout` (default `120` seconds). A timeout is not a kill; it lowers the mutation score like a survivor.",
  },
  {
    q: "How is the Gooze mutation score calculated?",
    a: "`score = killed / (all mutations - error)`. Only `killed` counts in your favor; `survived`, `not_covered`, and `timeout` lower it; `error` mutants (that did not compile) are excluded.",
  },
]


const faqJsonLd = faqPageJsonLd(faqs)
---
<DocsLayout metadata={metadata}>
    <section>
      <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />
      <h1 class="mt-0 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">Reading results</h1>
      <p class="gz-prose gz-prose--lead mb-8">Your run ended with <code>Mutation score: 84.62%</code>. Which mutants survived, and what
        should you change? Every mutation ends in one status. The statuses decide your mutation
        score and point to where your tests are weak.</p>
      <div>
        <div id="view">
          <h2>Open the results</h2>
          <p>After a run, view the reports. Gooze reads the reports directory and prints a per-file
            score table, one line per survivor, and the mutation score. For the <code>calc</code>{" "}package from the <a href="/docs/quick-start/">Quick start</a>:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze report view" />
          <Recording name="calc-report-view" title="Recorded gooze report view after the first run" />
          <p>Each survivor line gives the file, the line, the mutation operator, and the original and
            mutated code. Pass paths (<code>./internal/domain/...</code>, <code>calc.go</code>) to narrow it to
            some files, or <code>--all</code> to list every mutation with its diff.</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Keys and flags: <a href="/docs/cli/report/#view"><code>gooze report view</code></a>.
            File layout: <a href="/docs/reports/">Report format</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="statuses">
          <h2>Statuses</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><code>killed</code>: the tests of the package
              that owns the mutated file failed for the mutation
              (<code>go test -count=1</code> on that package).
              Usually good: your tests caught the change.</li>
            <li><code>survived</code>: the package tests still
              passed. Usually a weak or missing assertion.</li>
            <li><code>not_covered</code>: the mutated line is
              uncovered in the profile passed with{" "}<code>--coverage-profile</code>, so Gooze
              skipped its tests. It counts as a survivor (it lowers the score) but is tallied
              separately.</li>
            <li><code>timeout</code>: the tests exceeded{" "}<code>--mutation-timeout</code> (default <code>120</code>{" "}seconds). A timeout is not a kill.</li>
            <li><code>error</code>: the mutant did not
              compile; excluded from the score. In <code>v1.5.0+</code>, the plain output of{" "}<code>gooze run</code> prints only its status line, with no diff, and{" "}<a href="/docs/cli/report/#view-build-errors"><code>gooze report view</code></a> lists it under{" "}<code>Build errors</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>A missing test file does not skip anything: Gooze runs the sibling{" "}<code>&lt;name&gt;_test.go</code> if it exists, otherwise any{" "}<code>*_test.go</code> in the same package. If the package has no test file at all,
            the mutation is reported as <strong>survived</strong>.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="score">
          <h2>Mutation score</h2>
          <CodeBlock lang="text" code="score = killed / (all mutations - error)" />
          <ul>
            <li>Only <code>killed</code> counts in your favor.{" "}<code>survived</code>,{" "}<code>not_covered</code>, and{" "}<code>timeout</code> all lower the score.</li>
            <li><code>error</code> mutants (did not compile) are
              left out entirely. With no scorable mutations the score is <code>100%</code>.</li>
            <li><code>gooze run</code>,{" "}<code>gooze report merge</code>, and{" "}<code>gooze report view</code> print it as{" "}<code>Mutation score: NN.NN%</code> followed by a breakdown, for example{" "}<code>(11 killed, 2 survived)</code>. <code>gooze run</code> scores every result
              in its scope, cached and tested, and adds how many were which:{" "}<code>(11 killed, 2 survived; 0 cached, 13 tested)</code>. A run narrowed with{" "}<a href="/docs/usage/diff-mode/#scores"><code>--diff</code></a>,{" "}<a href="/docs/usage/single-function/"><code>--run</code> or{" "}<code>--mutagens</code></a> scores only its selection and
              names it at the end, for example{" "}<code>(13 killed, 0 survived; 0 cached, 13 tested; changed lines since main)</code>.
              To fail CI
              below a minimum, see{" "}<a href="/docs/ci/quality-gates/">quality gates</a>.</li>
            <li><code>_index.yaml</code> stores the raw counts but
              not the score; its <code>failed_mutations</code>{" "}lumps <code>error</code> and{" "}<code>timeout</code> together, so use the printed
              score rather than recomputing it.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div id="survivors">
          <h2>What to do when you see a survivor</h2>
          <p>A survivor is a hint: usually your tests don&apos;t check this behavior. For{" "}<code>calc.go:10</code>, no test calls <code>InRange</code> with <code>n</code> equal
            to <code>lo</code>, so <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&lt;=</code> give the same answers
            for every input the tests use.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Find it in <code>gooze report view</code> (file, line, original and mutated code), or
              open its full diff with <code>gooze report view --all</code>.</li>
            <li>Ask: what behavior changed? Each{" "}<a href="/docs/mutations/">mutation operator</a>{" "}explains what a survivor of its kind usually means.</li>
            <li>Add a test that would fail if this behavior changes. Here, the two cases on the
              bounds: <code>{"{0, 0, 10, true}"}</code> and <code>{"{10, 0, 10, true}"}</code>.</li>
            <li>Run Gooze again and check that it&apos;s killed now:</li>
          </ol>
          <Recording name="calc-fixed-run" title="Recorded last line of gooze run after adding the boundary tests" />
          <p>If the code should not be tested (generated code, debug helpers), skip it
            instead: see <a href="/docs/usage/ignoring-code/">Ignoring code</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <DocsFaq faqs={faqs} />
      </div>
    </section>
</DocsLayout>
